---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { LEGAL_UPDATED, OPERATOR_EMAIL, OPERATOR_NAME } from '../data/site';

const GOOGLE_PERMISSIONS_URL = 'https://myaccount.google.com/permissions';
// Limits of the hosted instance, set by the owner. They differ from the server defaults
// (120 and 30), so the deployment must set GCMCP_RATE_LIMIT_READS=50 and GCMCP_RATE_LIMIT_WRITES=20.
const READS_PER_MINUTE = 50;
const WRITES_PER_MINUTE = 20;
// Matches the 180-day purge in the server (internal/jobs).
const INACTIVITY_DAYS = 180;
// Owner: these terms are a plain-language draft written from how the service behaves.
// They are not legal advice. Have a lawyer read them if you want certainty.
// (A JS comment: an HTML comment would be published in the page.)
---

<Legal
  title="Terms of service"
  description="Terms of service of the hosted Google Contacts MCP service: who can use it, what you authorize, acceptable use, liability, and the governing law."
  path="/terms"
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <h2>Who provides the service</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided by {OPERATOR_NAME}, an individual (the operator). It connects an AI assistant you choose, such
    as Claude or ChatGPT, to your Google Contacts. Contact: <a href={`mailto:${OPERATOR_EMAIL}`}>{OPERATOR_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who can use it</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone with a Google account can connect. The operator can restrict the service to some e-mail domains, and can
    refuse an account that breaks these terms.
  </p>

  <h2>What you authorize</h2>
  <p>
    When you connect an assistant, you let it act on your Google Contacts through the Google People API: search, read,
    create, change, delete, and organize contacts in labels. You stay responsible for what you ask it to do. A deleted
    contact is deleted at Google and cannot be restored by this service, so keep your own export of your contacts.
  </p>

  <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Stay within the limits: {READS_PER_MINUTE} reading calls and {WRITES_PER_MINUTE} writing calls per user per minute.
    </li>
    <li>Do not try to reach another person's data, or to disrupt the service.</li>
    <li>Follow Google's terms for its APIs and the terms of your assistant's provider.</li>
    <li>Follow the law, including for the personal data of the people in your contacts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The service is provided as is</h2>
  <p>
    The service is free and comes with no promise of availability, of speed, or of backups. It can change, be interrupted
    or be shut down. If it is shut down, a notice appears on this website. Contacts changed by an assistant can be lost or
    overwritten by mistake.
  </p>

  <h2>Ending your use</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You can stop at any time: revoke access at <a href={GOOGLE_PERMISSIONS_URL}>myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>, or
      ask the operator to disconnect you.
    </li>
    <li>The operator can suspend an account that breaks these terms or harms the service.</li>
    <li>An account unused for {INACTIVITY_DAYS} days is purged automatically.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>What is kept about you, and for how long, is in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    As far as the law allows, the operator is not liable for indirect damage, or for the loss of data or of contacts, that
    results from using the service. Nothing here limits rights that the law does not allow to be limited, such as the
    rights of consumers.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
  <p>
    The date at the top of this page shows the last change. If you keep using the service after a change, you accept the
    new terms. If you do not, disconnect your account.
  </p>

  <h2>Governing law</h2>
  <p>These terms are governed by French law.</p>
</Legal>
